Q: How to plot real time graph of firebase data using react native? A: <p>I know it's an old question but I think the blank graph is happening because the Firebase data is being loaded inside render() and pushed into normal arrays. That code runs asynchronously, so by the time the callback fills sleeparr and datearr, the render has already finished. On top of that, those arrays get recreated every time render() runs, and pushing into them does not trigger a React re-render. That is why the hardcoded array works but the Firebase version stays blank.</p>
<p>So it'll be better not build the chart data inside render() at all. I would subscribe to Firebase once in componentDidMount(), convert the snapshot into fresh arrays, store those arrays in state with setState, and render the chart from state. That is the part that fixes the issue, not changing the date strings. The date strings are fine as axis labels here.</p>
<p>Something like this should work:</p>
<pre><code>import React from 'react';
import { View, Text, StyleSheet, Dimensions, ActivityIndicator } from 'react-native';
import { LineChart, XAxis, YAxis, Grid } from 'react-native-svg-charts';
// import db from your firebase db instance

class SleepPlot extends React.PureComponent {
  state = {
    sleepValues: [],
    dateLabels: [],
    loading: true,
  };

  componentDidMount() {
    this.symptomsRef = db.ref('symptoms');

    this.symptomsRef.on('value', (snapshot) =&gt; {
      const sleepValues = [];
      const dateLabels = [];

      snapshot.forEach((childSnapshot) =&gt; {
        const item = childSnapshot.val();

        if (item &amp;&amp; item.sleep != null &amp;&amp; item.date != null) {
          sleepValues.push(Number(item.sleep));
          dateLabels.push(String(item.date));
        }
      });

      this.setState({
        sleepValues,
        dateLabels,
        loading: false,
      });
    });
  }

  componentWillUnmount() {
    if (this.symptomsRef) {
      this.symptomsRef.off();
    }
  }

  render() {
    const { sleepValues, dateLabels, loading } = this.state;

    const axesSvg = { fontSize: 10, fill: '#008384', fontWeight: '400' };
    const verticalContentInset = { top: 20, bottom: 20 };
    const xAxisHeight = 50;

    if (loading) {
      return &lt;ActivityIndicator size=&quot;large&quot; color=&quot;#00bcd4&quot; /&gt;;
    }

    if (sleepValues.length === 0) {
      return &lt;Text&gt;No data found.&lt;/Text&gt;;
    }

    return (
      &lt;View style={styles.container}&gt;
        &lt;Text style={styles.heading}&gt;Sleep Graph&lt;/Text&gt;

        &lt;View style={{ height: 250, paddingHorizontal: 16, flexDirection: 'row' }}&gt;
          &lt;YAxis
            data={sleepValues}
            contentInset={verticalContentInset}
            svg={axesSvg}
            numberOfTicks={5}
          /&gt;

          &lt;View style={{ flex: 1, marginLeft: 10 }}&gt;
            &lt;LineChart
              style={{ flex: 1 }}
              data={sleepValues}
              contentInset={verticalContentInset}
              svg={{ stroke: '#00bcd4', strokeWidth: 2 }}
            &gt;
              &lt;Grid /&gt;
            &lt;/LineChart&gt;

            &lt;XAxis
              style={{ height: xAxisHeight, marginHorizontal: -10 }}
              data={sleepValues}
              formatLabel={(value, index) =&gt; dateLabels[index]}
              contentInset={{ left: 10, right: 10 }}
              svg={{ ...axesSvg, rotation: 25, originY: 20, y: 5 }}
            /&gt;
          &lt;/View&gt;
        &lt;/View&gt;
      &lt;/View&gt;
    );
  }
}

export default SleepPlot;

const styles = StyleSheet.create({
  container: {
    flex: 1,
    backgroundColor: '#d6ffff',
    alignItems: 'center',
    justifyContent: 'center',
    height: Dimensions.get('window').height,
    width: Dimensions.get('window').width,
  },
  heading: {
    color: '#00bcd4',
    fontWeight: '400',
    fontSize: 20,
    marginBottom: 20,
  },
});
</code></pre>
<p>Also it would be better not to use this.state.someArray.push(...) in the Firebase callback. That mutates state directly, which React will not handle correctly. Build new arrays first, then call setState once with the finished result. Also, if you had a loop like for (let x = 0; x &lt;= array.length; x++), that goes one past the end and should be &lt; array.length instead. Those were part of the problems in the edited version too.</p>
<p>So, move the Firebase listener out of render(), put the fetched values into state, and render the chart from state. Once you do that, the graph should render correctly with live Firebase data just like it does with the static array.</p>
